  <content>
  <section><title>Factoring Quadratic Equations Without a Linear Term</title>
<para>We can use what we have learned in the previous lesson to write quadratic equations of a special form into factored form.</para>
<para>Particularly, we can rewrite:</para>
<para>\(x^2− c^2\) into \((x + c)(x − c)\).</para>
<para>Let's look at an example.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Write \(v^2−36\) in factored form.</para>
<para>We know that the form we are looking for is \((v\; −\) __ \()(v \;+\) __ \()\).</para>
<para>Since we know the square <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">root</term> of \(v^2\) is \(v\) and the square root of 36 is 6, the factored form is \((v − 6)(v + 6)\).</para>
<para>We can multiply to check our work.</para>
<para>\((v − 6)(v + 6)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{=v^2+6v-6v-36\\=v^2-36\; {\style{color:red}\checkmark}}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
<para>The process is similar when there is a coefficient on the squared term.</para>
<para>\(a^2x^2−c^2\;\;\;\;\) factors into \(\;\;\;\;(ax + c)(ax − c)\)</para>
<para>Let's look at another example to understand the process.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Write \(16m^2− 81\) in factored form.</para>
<para>The form we are looking for now is ( __ − __ )( __ + __ ).</para>
<para>Taking the square root of the first term gives us \(4m\).</para>
<para>So, we have \((4m\; −\) __ \()(4m\; +\) __ \()\).</para>
<para>Now, we are in the same place as in the previous example. The square root of 81 is 9.</para>
<para>So, the factored form is \((4m − 9 )(4m + 9)\).</para>
<para>Again, we can multiply to check.</para>
<para>\((4m − 9)(4m + 9)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{= 4m^2 + 36m − 36m − 81\\= 4m^2 − 81\; {\style{color:red}\checkmark}}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para>As we see in the activity, where the variable is located is not important as long as the quadratic binomial is still the difference of two squares.</para>
<para>Write \(64 − 9b^2\) in factored form.</para>
<para>We use the same concept to find the factored form.</para>
<para>\(64 − 9b^2= (8 − 3b)(8 + 3b)\)</para>
<para>We can multiply to check.</para>
<para>\((8 − 3b)(8 + 3b)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{= 64 + 24b − 24b − 9b^2\\= 64 − 9b^2\; {\style{color:red}\checkmark}}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Remember that any expression of the form \(x^2 + c\) or \(ax^2+ c\) does not have a factored form!</para>
<list>
  <item> For \(x^2+ c\), the \(c\) is not squared, and the terms are not being subtracted. </item>
  <item> For \(ax^2+ c\), neither the \(a\) nor the \(c\) is a perfect square, and the terms are not being subtracted. </item>
</list>
<note class="try"><title>Try It: Factoring Quadratic Equations Without a Linear Term</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write \(x^2− 4\) in factored form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to write the expressions in factored form.</para>
  
  <para>\((x − 2)(x + 2)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></note>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>

    <para>Write \(100x^2− 121\) in factored form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to write the expressions in factored form.</para>
  
  <para>\((10x − 11)(10x + 11)\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
